course: dictionary
lesson: 7171
title: Permanently: for ever, and always
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Water ice exists in permanently shadowed craters near the Moon's poles.» The word permanently tells you the shadow never goes away. The craters stay dark for ever, so the ice stays too. Permanently means for ever, or for a very long time, without changing or ending.
explain: The word also works for things that cannot be undone. Burning wood changes it permanently: the wood cannot go back to how it was. A permanent marker writes words that stay on paper and will not wash off. When you see permanent, ask: will this ever change back?
explain: Grown-ups sometimes use permanently in a looser way. «Permanently tired» means tired all the time, which is an exaggeration to show how exhausted someone feels. Read the other words in the sentence to check which meaning fits. In a science text, the meaning is almost always for ever.
fact: Permanently is an adverb, from the adjective permanent.
fact: «Permanently» means for ever, or for a very long time, without changing or ending.
fact: A permanent change cannot be reversed, like wood that has been burned.
fact: «Permanently damaged» means damaged in a way that cannot be fixed.
fact: Informally, «permanently tired» means tired all the time, which is an exaggeration.
exercise: «Water ice exists in permanently shadowed craters near the Moon's poles.» What does permanently mean here? | Very quickly | Only at night | * Always in shadow, never lit by sunlight | Sometimes, now and then
exercise: «Adoption is when a child joins a new family permanently because their birth parents can't bring them up.» What does permanently show? | For a day, then home again | Until the child is bored | Only for one weekend | * For ever, not just for a visit
exercise: A permanent marker writes words that will not wash off. What does permanent mean here? | Brightly coloured | Very cheap | * Lasting for ever, cannot be removed | Made of wood
test: «Scott's and Shackleton's huts still stand after over 100 years, and no people live in Antarctica permanently.» What does permanently mean in this sentence? | Very quietly | Only for a short stay | * For ever, as a full-time home | Mostly in summer
test: Burning wood changes it permanently. Can the wood go back to how it was? | Yes, it goes back when it cools | Only if you wait a day | * No, the change cannot be reversed | It depends on the weather
exam: Ella says, «The shop is permanently closed.» What does she mean? | It is closed for a short while | * It has closed for good and will not reopen | It is closed for lunch | It is closed on Sundays only
